{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1785240781,"nonce":"ZdFt1gFKvnqcsRItYp43KyNmwQE=","target":"ec87cbcb4589c19d07f84345f7d8da423fddafbeadd25ad33a46e3ab17b5b495","message":"Reported by Mark Brown on IRC, 2026-07-27. Mark ran into trouble with\na cross-tree series where he acked the patches for his subsystem and\nthe maintainer expected to apply the whole series dropped the ball; he\nnoted there are quite a few UI issues around cross-tree work in\ngeneral.\n\nUmbrella entry for cross-tree workflow gaps in b4 review.\n\nThe core problem: the series lifecycle (new -\u003e replied -\u003e waiting -\u003e\naccepted -\u003e thanked -\u003e archived) assumes the person tracking the series\nis the applying maintainer. In a cross-tree series, a maintainer often\nplays a different role: they review and ack the patches touching their\nsubsystem, and some other maintainer is expected to take the whole\nseries through their tree.\n\nThere is no state that expresses \"done on my end, travelling through\nsomeone else's tree -- keep watching for resends\". Current options all\nfit badly:\n\n- leave it active: clutters the list indefinitely for a series you are\n  not going to apply;\n- snooze: defers, but expresses \"remind me later\", not \"hand-off\", and\n  a wake-up is time/tag-based rather than resend-based;\n- archive: loses the series entirely, and if the other maintainer drops\n  the ball and the submitter resends, the resend is swallowed (see the\n  unarchive bug, filed separately);\n- partial (from bug a01c5b5): covers \"I cherry-picked my bits into my\n  own tree\", which is a related but different cross-tree flavour --\n  here nothing gets applied to the local tree at all.\n\nFirst concrete candidate: a hand-off status (working name: \"acked\" or\n\"external\") that behaves like 'waiting' for revision discovery -- the\nseries sits in a low-priority group, 'u' keeps checking lore, and a\nnewly discovered revision promotes it back to active review. That is\nexactly the trigger broonie needed: the resend of the dropped series\nwould have woken the entry up instead of vanishing.\n\nOpen questions / next steps:\n\n- broonie says there are \"quite a few UI issues around cross tree\n  stuff\"; we have exactly one datapoint. Ask him to enumerate the\n  others before designing anything bigger.\n- how a hand-off state should interact with Patchwork state sync (we\n  should probably not touch the remote state at all -- the applying\n  maintainer owns it);\n- whether thanks/archive should ever be offered for a hand-off series\n  (probably archive only, once it lands in the other tree).","files":null}]}